# 10. AUTHENTIC AUDIO ENGINE

Audio is a core functional requirement.

Use real recordings from an identified male qari, with lawful access and appropriate attribution.

Do not use synthetic text-to-speech as a substitute for Quran recitation.

## Audio resource model

Distinguish between:

- Complete-surah audio.
- Individual-verse audio.
- Any provider-supported segmented audio.

Do not infer that a full-surah recording contains independently addressable verse files.

For each audio resource, track the metadata actually supplied or verified by the provider:

- Reciter identifier.
- Surah and verse identifiers where applicable.
- Actual audio URL or provider resource identifier.
- Duration when available.
- Format when available.
- Source attribution.
- License information.
- Verification and availability status.

Do not invent duration or coverage metadata.

## Required playback behavior

Implement:

- Play and pause.
- Real elapsed time and duration.
- Seek when the resource supports seeking.
- Previous and next verse where supported.
- Repeat current verse when a genuine verse-level recording exists.
- Continuous surah playback.
- Reciter selection where multiple compatible reciters are available.
- Loading and buffering indicators.
- Failed playback state and retry.
- Playback-speed control where supported.
- Responsive player controls.
- Current-verse highlighting.
- Proper cleanup of media resources and event listeners.
- Prevention of unintended overlapping playback.

When advancing between verse-level files, maintain predictable queue behavior and handle missing audio assets.

When only a full-surah file is available, provide surah-level playback honestly. Do not claim accurate verse-level seeking or repeat unless the required timing or segmentation data exists.

Use native HTML audio or an established, justified player implementation.

Do not manually fetch the entire audio file into application memory merely to implement a seek bar.

Support HTTP range requests through the actual hosting provider where applicable.

Respect browser autoplay restrictions and require a user gesture where needed.

Background playback is best-effort and depends on browser and operating-system support.

If playback speed changes pitch or affects recitation quality, document the limitation and use the most appropriate supported implementation.

Do not claim that a speed control preserves natural recitation unless that behavior has been verified.

Audio must never block the initial rendering of Arabic text.

